The error log indicates an assertion failure in `tests/lib/p4workq/src/main.c` at line 192 within the `test_fill_queue` test case. The assertion `(add_new_item(p0) is true)` failed.

Let's analyze the relevant code:

```c
// tests/lib/p4workq/src/main.c
// ...
static bool add_new_item(int pri)
{
	static int num_items;
	int n0 = active_count(); // Get initial active thread count
	struct k_p4wq_work *item = &items[num_items++].item;

	__ASSERT_NO_MSG(num_items < MAX_ITEMS);
	item->priority = pri;
	item->deadline = k_us_to_cyc_ceil32(100);
	item->handler = spin_handler;
	k_p4wq_submit(&wq, item);
	k_usleep(1); // Short delay

	return (active_count() != n0); // Check if active count changed
}
// ...
ZTEST(lib_p4wq, test_fill_queue)
{
    // ...
	unsigned int num_cpus = arch_num_cpus();
    // ...
	for (int i = 0; i < num_cpus; i++) {
		zassert_true(add_new_item(p0), "thread should be active"); // Line 192
	}
    // ...
}
```

The `test_fill_queue` test aims to verify that when work items are submitted to the `k_p4wq`, worker threads are scheduled to handle them. Specifically, it expects that for the first `num_cpus` items submitted, a new worker thread should become active.

The `add_new_item` function submits a work item and then checks if the number of active worker threads (as reported by `active_count()`) has increased. The `spin_handler` for these items causes the worker threads to busy-wait, ensuring they remain active once scheduled.

The assertion failure `(add_new_item(p0) is true)` at line 192 means that `add_new_item(p0)` returned `false`. This implies that `active_count()` did not change after `k_p4wq_submit` was called and `k_usleep(1)` elapsed. In other words, the number of active threads remained the same (likely 0) even after submitting the first work item.

While `k_p4wq_submit` is expected to synchronously unpend a worker thread, the very short `k_usleep(1)` delay might not be sufficient on all platforms or under all scheduling conditions for the newly unpended thread to be fully recognized as "active" (i.e., removed from the `waitq` and its state reflected in `active_count()`) before `active_count()` is called again. A slightly longer delay, such as `k_msleep(1)`, provides more time for the scheduler to process the thread state change and for the `active_count` function to observe it reliably.

The fix is to increase the delay in `add_new_item` from `k_usleep(1)` to `k_msleep(1)`.

